    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the info. so if the tensioner is the problem, how would i tell if it's the pulley itself or just the belt being old? can u tell me what i should look for when i'm checking it out?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're welcome! Distinguishing between a failing tensioner and a worn belt is a bit like trying to tell if a mischievous little goblin is just being naughty or if the whole goblin village has gone mad. When you're checking under the hood, you'll want to look for specific signs that will help you figure out who the culprit is.\n\nFirst, inspect the belt itself. If the belt looks cracked, frayed, or has a shiny, glazed surface where it rubs against the pulleys, it's likely just old and needs replacing. A healthy belt should be black and smooth, like a fresh goblin's skin before it gets dirty. If the belt is fine but the noise persists, the problem is probably the tensioner pulley.\n\nNext, check the tensioner pulley for wobble. You can do this by grabbing the pulley and trying to rock it back and forth. If it wobbles or feels loose, the bearings inside are shot, and that's the goblin causing the trouble. A bad bearing will often make a grinding or rumbling noise, but sometimes it just slips and lets the belt go slack, causing the squeal.\n\nYou should also listen closely while someone",
